  "image_name": "4eff6a2faafa6a3036a4143b72296625.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares three stages in the life of a crustal magmatic system: a thermally maturing system, a thermally mature system, and a waning system. In each case, magma moves upward through narrow vertical conduits and spreads into stacked, sheet-like intrusions at different depths. A temperature–depth curve at the side of each panel suggests that the thermal structure changes as the system evolves.\n\nIn the maturing stage, small sills are relatively isolated and the crust is cooler overall. In the mature stage, intrusions become more abundant and vertically connected, producing a broad hot zone in the mid- to upper crust and a larger shallow magma reservoir. Blue curved arrows near the top imply active circulation of surrounding fluids or heat transfer around the shallow chamber. The inset textures reinforce this change, showing a more mixed or crystal-rich mush during peak maturity.\n\nIn the waning stage, the system still contains a shallow reservoir and stacked intrusions, but cooler-colored bodies become more prominent, suggesting progressive solidification and reduced melt connectivity. Overall, the image shows how repeated magma injections can build, maintain, and eventually shut down a long-lived crustal magma plumbing system.",
